	---
NAR Plugin
	---
	---
Mark Donszelmann
	---
	
Introduction
 
	NOTE: <<bold>> print is <<NOT implemented yet>>.

	This plugin for Maven 2 allows you to compile native code (c++, c and fortran) on
a number of different architectures (Linux, Windows, MacOSX, Solaris, ...) and with
a number of different compilers/linkers (g++, Microsoft Visual C++, CC, ...)
The output produced is wrapped up in Native ARchive files (.nar) some of which
are machine independent (-noarch), while others are machine specific and thus depend
on a combination of machine architecture(A), operating-system(O) and linker(L) identified
as AOL.
These nar files can be installed in the local maven repository and deployed to a 
standard maven (web) server, using the standard maven-install-plugin and maven-deploy-plugin.

	Other maven projects may specify dependencies on these nar files using the standard
maven dependency declaration. Nar files get downloaded, unpacked and 
installed in the local maven repository, just like jar files are (apart from the
unpacking).
    
	The NAR plugin executes the following goals, in the order below, to create and deploy nar files. 
The goals are part of the
<<<nar>>> packaging/{{{./lifecycle.html}lifecycle}}, which inserts the nar goals into the standard lifecyle. Using nar packaging
allows you to build a jar file as well as nar files. The list below shows the sequence of the NAR goals:

    [[1]] {{{nar-validate}nar-validate}}
    
    [[2]] {{{nar-download}nar-download}}
    
    [[3]] {{{nar-unpack}nar-unpack}}
    
    [[4]] {{{nar-gnu-configure}nar-gnu-configure}}
    
    [[5]] {{{nar-system-generate}nar-system-generate}}
    
    [[6]] {{{nar-resources}nar-resources}}
    
    [[7]] {{{nar-gnu-resources}nar-gnu-resources}}
    
    [[8]] {{{nar-javah}nar-javah}}
    
    [[9]] {{{nar-gnu-make}nar-gnu-make}}

    [[10]] {{{nar-compile}nar-compile}}
    
    [[11]] {{{nar-gnu-process}nar-gnu-process}}
    
    [[12]] {{{nar-test-unpack}nar-test-unpack}}
    
    [[13]] {{{nar-testCompile}nar-testCompile}}
   
    [[14]] {{{nar-test}nar-test}}
    
    [[15]] {{{nar-package}nar-package}}
    
    [[16]] {{{nar-integration-test}nar-integration-test}}
    
    [[17]] {{{install}install (standard maven goal)}}
    
    [[18]] {{{deploy}deploy (standard maven goal)}}

	[]
	
* Initialization

	The NAR Plugin starts off by setting and deducing all kinds of property values for usage
in the goals below. The configuration section of the NAR plugin allows one to override 
most of the default settings. The default settings come from the AOL properties file 
which allows us to specify different defaults depending on the architecture-os-linker
combination, see {{{./aol.html}AOL Properties}}.
               
	The NAR Plugin tries to deduce the "Operating System"
and the "Architecture" of the machine. Both can be overridden by setting <<<os>>>
and <<<arch>>> in the {{{./configuration.html}configuration}}.
                
	The name of the linker is looked up in an architecture-os specific way, but can be overridden
by setting <<<linker.name>>> (this means the subtag \<name\> of the \<linker\> tag in the
configuration section). Now that the linker name is known
all other properties are looked up with a prefix of <<<[arch.[os.[linker.]]]>>> from the AOL Properties
file, but can be overridden in the configuration section (which can be made AOL specific by
putting it inside a profile).
 
* {nar-validate}
                
    The NAR plugin makes sure the linker name can be found and that at least one compiler exists
for this linker. It further checks the version number of the linker to see if the linker is actually
found on the path.               
   
* {nar-download}

	Your NAR artifact(s) may be dependent on other nar artifacts. The standard maven dependency 
declaration in the POM is used to describe such dependencies of type "nar", see {{{./narDependencies.html}
NAR dependencies}}. By the time this goal is running maven will have already downloaded 
all dependent jar and nar files some of which may include a nar.properties file. 
This property file contains information on what other machine dependent and machine independent nar files to download.
This goal will download any further necessary nar files into the local repository.

* {nar-unpack}

	Since a nar file is of no use to any native compilation process, the nar-unpack goal unpacks the nar into 
the "nar" subdirectory of the target directory. The dependencies taken in account are those with the scopes compile, 
provided and system which are the scopes needed for compilation.

        
* {nar-gnu-configure} 

    If you store files in the <gnuSourceDirectory> (src/gnu) the NAR plugin will try to run autogen and configure
over them with a target set to <gnuTargetDirectory>. This process can be disabled by setting <gnuAutogenSkip> and or 
<gnuConfigureSkip>. On Windows, this process will only run if <gnuUseOnWindows> is set. The generated and configured
files are all stored in <gnuTargetDirectory> and will be handled in subsequent NAR goals.    
        
* {nar-system-generate}

    This goal generates a NarSystem class if necessary. This class contains helper methods for your
code to handle things at run-time. The static method "loadLibrary()" is currently the only method
generated and will load your JNI library with name "artifactId-version". This goal is only executed
if you produce a JNI library and you specify a <narSystemPackage> as subtag of <library>. The NarSystem
class will then be created in this package.
                                            
* {nar-resources}

	This is an optional goal. It will take any resources in src/nar/resources and copy them into
specific areas under target/nar. Resources may include pre-compiled/linked libraries and other 
shareable items. This goal can be used to create a nar library from a distribution that comes
with compiled shareable libraries.
  
* {nar-gnu-resources}

    Copies the <resourceIncludeDir> (include) from the <gunSourceDirectory> (src/gnu) into the
noarch area in the target directory.
                
* {nar-javah}

    Generates JNI headers from compiled classes in <<<javah.classDirectory>>> (by
 default <<<target/classes>>>), selecting native classes with <<<javah.includes>>>
 and <<<javah.excludes>>>. Sources are not required. Headers are published to
 <<<javah.jniDirectory>>> (by default <<<target/nar/javah-include>>>).

    The goal remains <<<nar-javah>>> and its configuration remains <<<javah>>>
 with every JDK. With the default <<<mode>>> of <<<auto>>> and <<<name>>> of
 <<<javah>>>, the usual tool selection is:

*--------------------+-----------------------------------+-------------------------------+
| Selected JDK       | Available header tools            | Tool used in <<<auto>>> mode   |
*--------------------+-----------------------------------+-------------------------------+
| JDK 8              | <<<javah>>> and <<<javac -h>>>     | <<<javah>>>                   |
*--------------------+-----------------------------------+-------------------------------+
| JDK 9              | <<<javah>>> and <<<javac -h>>>     | <<<javah>>>                   |
*--------------------+-----------------------------------+-------------------------------+
| JDK 10 and later   | <<<javac -h>>>                    | <<<javac -h>>>                |
*--------------------+-----------------------------------+-------------------------------+

    <<<javac -h>>> has been available since JDK 8; <<<javah>>> was removed in
 JDK 10. See the
 {{{https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/migrate/removed-tools-and-components.html}JDK migration guide}}.
 Selection checks tool availability, so a JDK 8 or 9 toolchain exposing only
 <<<javac>>> also uses <<<javac -h>>>. The <<<javac>>> backend requires JDK 8 or newer.

    For the standard tool names, the selected Maven JDK toolchain supplies the
 header tool. Without a selected toolchain, NAR uses <<<javaHome>>>, which defaults to a location derived from the
 JVM running Maven. A selected toolchain is authoritative: a missing tool does
 not cause NAR to search another JDK. The project's Java source/target/release
 settings do not select the header tool. For example, a JDK 21 toolchain uses
 <<<javac -h>>> even when the application targets Java 8.

    <<<javah>>> reads compiled classes directly. <<<javac -h>>> generates headers
 while compiling Java source, so NAR uses ASM to read the compiled classes and
 reconstruct minimal temporary source declarations. It then compiles those
 declarations with the selected <<<javac>>>. Original application and dependency
 sources are not needed, and application method bodies are not reconstructed.

    Select the backend with <<<javah.mode>>>:

    * <<<auto>>> (default): use <<<javah>>> when the selected JDK provides it;
 otherwise generate temporary Java declarations using ASM and run <<<javac -h>>>.

    * <<<javah>>>: require the legacy tool and retain timestamp-based generation.

    * <<<javac>>>: require JDK 8 or newer and always regenerate all selected headers,
 including dependency classes, so dependency changes are taken into account.

    []

    A custom <<<javah.name>>> (a value other than <<<javah>>>) remains a legacy
 command in <<<auto>>> or <<<javah>>> mode. A failing command is reported; it does
 not trigger fallback. <<<javac>>> mode ignores <<<javah.name>>>; select this mode
 to use the new backend on JDK 8 or 9 as well as on newer JDKs. Put the following
 fragment inside the NAR plugin's <<<configuration>>> element:

+--
<javah>
  <mode>javac</mode>
  <extraClasses>
    <extraClass>example.NativeLibrary$Api</extraClass>
    <extraClass>example.Constants</extraClass>
  </extraClasses>
</javah>
+--

    The compile classpath supplies dependency types; <<<javah.classPaths>>> can
 replace it explicitly. <<<javah.bootClassPaths>>> is also passed to the compiler
 (subject to that JDK's supported options). The new backend supports directories,
 JARs and multi-release JARs, using the selected compiler's JDK version. Local
 JAR and directory entries in a JAR manifest's <<<Class-Path>>> are resolved
 relative to that JAR and searched immediately after it, including transitive
 entries. Duplicate entries and cycles are ignored; remote URLs are not fetched.
 Manifest entries follow the selected compiler: JDK 8-10 treat them as file names,
 while JDK 11 and later decode file URLs (including percent-encoded spaces).
 With the <<<javac>>> backend, <<<javah.extraClasses>>> uses binary class names
 and works even with no local native classes. In legacy <<<javah>>> mode, extra
 classes are passed to the tool only when local native classes trigger generation.
 Explicitly selected classes without native methods receive a
 constants-only header, without an extra generated constant.

    The <<<javac>>> backend supports application and dependency top-level and member
 classes, including nested classes and native enums. Local/anonymous classes and
 classes enclosed within them cannot be reconstructed as header targets. JDK/platform
 classes cannot be header targets (targets such as <<<java.sql.Types>>> require
 legacy <<<javah>>> from a JDK that still provides it); ordinary references to
 JDK types are supported. Missing or source-inexpressible required declarations
 produce errors.

    Native signatures, primitive constants, containment and superclass/interface
 relationships are reconstructed. Real signature types and superclasses must be
 available on the classpath. Types used only in omitted methods or unrelated fields are not eagerly
 resolved. Superclass constructors must have an accessible, source-expressible
 invocation; failures identify the class and constructor candidates. Checked
 constructor exceptions are specialized for the selected superclass type and the
 emitted invocation's inferred type arguments. Unconstrained throws-only type
 variables can infer <<<RuntimeException>>>; unchecked exceptions require no throws
 clause. Dependent bounds retain checked coverage needed by older compilers. Inaccessible or shadowed checked exception
 names use the nearest usable superclass, including when a default-package name
 conflicts with an import. Types from the default package are never imported.
 Name checks verify the actual class denoted by declared and inherited member
 names, rejecting hidden or ambiguous spellings while retaining valid self and
 member references. Verified lexical bindings take precedence over canonical
 names, so an inherited public member can be used without naming its inaccessible
 declaring class. When a lexical name is hidden or unavailable, the generator
 looks for an accessible subtype qualifier whose member resolves to the same
 binary class. Parameterized member spellings preserve the declaring owner's type
 arguments by projecting candidate qualifiers through their generic ancestry;
 candidates must satisfy their bounds and denote the exact owner instantiation.
 Wildcard upper bounds must permit a common subtype with the qualifier's bounds,
 including compatible generic ancestors; wildcard lower bounds must satisfy those
 bounds. Incompatible candidates are skipped. When a required qualifier is itself
 generated, the generator retains its necessary class and enclosing type parameters
 rather than erasing a member type that dependency contracts still parameterize.
 Native signatures in these declarations retain the necessary generic source form.
 Compiled native descriptors are checked against the original class files before
 headers are published.
 Constructor arguments and checked exceptions use these same
 spelling rules. Qualifier discovery first uses known metadata; if necessary,
 it indexes superclass/interface names from classpath files, respecting classpath
 precedence, manifest entries and the compiler's multi-release JAR view. This
 fallback does not resolve unrelated method or field types. Superclass and
 interface clauses use their declaration scope;
 member names enter scope in the class body. Unrelated member class files are not
 resolved to perform these checks.
 Generated subclasses follow the throws clause actually emitted for their parent;
 a parent constructor choice is retained only if its generated descendants can
 express the resulting clauses. Rejected choices discard descendant imports and
 references before another parent candidate is considered. Constructor selection checks
 exception and argument spellings against the compilation unit's required names,
 trying another candidate when a throws clause cannot be expressed. Rejected
 candidates do not retain imports. Throwaway constructors retain required type parameters and all their bounds when casting
 arguments to generic superclass constructors, using fresh names to avoid shadowing
 classes or package names. Fresh type parameter names reserve identifiers in the
 actual selected spellings, including discovered qualifiers; subsequent name
 resolution also avoids type parameters already in scope. Fully typed constructor calls are preferred. When
 constructor argument types or generic bounds cannot be named due to accessibility
 or source-name conflicts, calls using raw casts are tried before calls requiring
 bare null arguments, avoiding ambiguous overloads when a usable cast is available.
 Null inference remains available for inaccessible constructor bounds. Constructor
 calls are selected from class metadata before compilation, checking applicability,
 generic bounds and overload specificity. Wildcard capture includes parameterized
 enclosing types, even when the member itself is not generic. All parameters are supplied, including an
 array for a varargs parameter, and the call must be applicable without boxing or
 varargs expansion. Ambiguous calls are rejected during selection; failures identify
 the competing constructor descriptors. All generated units are then compiled
 together in one javac invocation. Compiler failures propagate without retrying.
 Enclosing
 interfaces retain interface form. Enclosing records retain their record form
 without reconstructing record components. Overrides needed to resolve conflicting
 interface methods are retained. Records and enums receive throwaway method bodies
 where needed to satisfy interface contracts, choosing covariant return types
 independently of interface order. Return comparison includes generic arguments,
 wildcard bounds and parameterized supertypes. Wildcard projection retains bounds
 declared on the generic type, including dependent and intersection bounds. Redundant interface clauses are omitted so that
 inherited generic instantiations, such as <<<Comparable<E\>>>> on an enum, remain
 authoritative. Interface arguments on non-raw declarations and supporting interface
 type parameters are retained, including bounds involving other generated classes.
 Superclass arguments are retained consistently with interface arguments, constructor
 calls and inherited-method lookup. When a supporting generic superclass is erased,
 interface paths sharing its raw ancestors are reconciled with that erasure.
 Enclosing type arguments are kept separate from member type parameters
 when matching inherited methods. Retained generic contracts are specialized before
 supporting methods are emitted: for example, <<<Supplier<String>.get()>>> needs a <<<String>>> return.
 Method type parameters, their bounds and parameterized signature types are retained
 when needed for valid supporting overrides. Compiler-generated bridge adapters do
 not replace source overrides. Inherited methods are checked against the reconstructed
 superclass declarations, with generic throws clauses specialized alongside return
 and parameter types. Supporting return types satisfy both the retained superclass
 method and all retained interface contracts. Native JVM descriptors remain unchanged. Unrelated methods remain omitted. Required contract signature types,
 including generic argument types needed by the compiler's inheritance checks, must
 be available on the classpath.

    Temporary sources, throwaway class files, staged headers, compiler arguments and
 diagnostics are kept in <<<target/nar/javac-headers>>> (relative to the configured
 build directory), in <<<sources>>>, <<<classes>>>, <<<headers>>>, <<<javac.args>>>
 and <<<javac.log>>>. Diagnostics from the single compilation are retained in
 <<<javac.log>>> and are not parsed to select constructors. Temporary outputs are
 cleared before compilation.
 Application class files are not overwritten, and annotation
 processing is disabled. Source names account for same-package types and reserve
 unnamed-package references before adding imports. After the single compilation,
 native method names, descriptors and static/instance flags are compared with the
 original class files. This check and verification of every requested header
 finish before publication; unsuccessful generation leaves existing headers intact.

* {nar-gnu-make}  

    Runs "make" followed by "make install" to put the final files in position for copying to the NAR target areas by the 
nar-gnu-process goal.
                
* {nar-compile}
                
	This goal will compile the native source code (c, c++ or fortran) and archive it into a shared library.
You can also produce a jni or a static library by setting <<<library.type>>>.
To handle the variety of compilers and linkers, the NAR plugin uses a derivative of the cpptasks from the ant-contrib
project, with some minor improvements and additions of compilers. Most of the settings for cpptasks, such as compiler,
linker, options and include dirs are available through the NAR plugin, see {{{./configuration.html}configuration}}.

	The NAR plugin searches the directory structure under COMPILER.sourceDirectory, which defaults to
src/main. The standard way to separate your sources for different languages would be src/main/c, src/main/c++
and src/main/fortran, but any file under src/main is searched for.

	The nar plugin will automatically select the correct compiler (c, c++ or fortran) based on the type of
source, as specified in the patterns in <<<AOL.c.includes>>>, 
<<<AOL.cpp.includes>>> and <<<AOL.fortran.includes>>>, where AOL is a dotted qualifier of the architecture,
os and linker (<<<x86.Windows.msvc>>> for example).

	Include paths are added in this order from:

    * any directories set in <<<COMPILER.includePaths>>>, where COMPILER is c, cpp or fortran.

    * the <<<javah.jniDirectory>>>.

    * if <<<java.include>>> is true or if <<<javah.jniDirectory>>>
    exists we add one of the following, relative to the java home directory:
    
    	* <<<java.includePaths>>> if set,
    	
    	* otherwise the <<<AOL.java.include>>> property from the AOL properties.
    	
    	[]
    
    
    * the header files of any of the nar type dependencies, unless it is a jni library.
    
    * any system directories set in <<<COMPILER.sysincludepath>>>, where COMPILER is c, cpp or fortran.

	[]

	The static or dynamic library is linked against:

    * the list of libraries set in <<<linker.libs>>>. Using nar dependencies is preferred though.
    
    * the libraries of any of the nar type dependencies.
    
    * the java virtual machine if you set
    <<<java.link>>> to true. The location of the runtime can be overridden in <<<java.runtimeDirectory>>>.

	[]

	All include files from <<<COMPILER.includePaths>>> are copied to be included
in the noarch nar file.

	<< TBD If you set maven.nar.includefilesonly to true the compilation step will be 
skipped. This flag can be used if you want to distribute a library that only contains pure
abstract classes in include files.>>
                
* {nar-gnu-process}  

    Copies the gnu "install"-ed area into the NAR target area for testing and packing. 
                
* {nar-test-unpack}  

    Unpacks the nar dependencies into the "test-nar" subdirectory of the target directory. The dependencies taken in 
account are those with the scopes compile, provided, runtime, system and test which are the scopes needed for tests 
compilation and executions.
                
* {nar-testCompile}

	This goal will compile the native test source code (c, c++ or fortran) and create executables from it.
To handle the variety of compilers and linkers, the NAR plugin uses a derivative of the cpptasks from the ant-contrib
project, with some minor improvements and additions of compilers.  Most of the settings for cpptasks, such as compiler,
linker, options and include dirs are available through the NAR plugin, see {{{./configuration.html}configuration}}.

	The NAR plugin searches the directory structure under COMPILER.sourceDirectory, which defaults to
src/test. The standard way to separate your sources for different languages would be src/test/c, src/test/c++
and src/test/fortran, but any file under src/test is searched for.

	The compiler will automatically select the correct compiler (c, c++ or fortran) based on the type of
sources, as specified in the patterns in <<<AOL.c.includes>>>, 
<<<AOL.nar.src.includes>>> and <<<AOL.fortran.includes>>>, where AOL is a dotted qualifier of the architecture,
os and linker (<<<x86.Windows.msvc>>> for example).
                
	<<TBD If you set maven.nar.includefilesonly to true the compile-tests goal will be 
skipped.>>


* {nar-test}
           
	Runs any native tests as well as any executables that have been produced.
                
* {nar-package}                
   
	This goal creates the artifact jar/nar file(s). The jar file is created by the standard package goal.
The following jar and nar files are created:

    * \<Artifact\>-\<version\>.jar, containing a property file describing the behaviour of the 
    library and other nar files available. For a description see {{{./narDependencies.html}
    NAR dependencies}}. This jar file is renamed to nar when installed or deployed.
    
    * \<Artifact\>-\<version\>-noarch.nar, a compressed jar file containing non machine
    specific parts of the distribution, such as the include directories.
    
    * \<Artifact\>-\<version\>-\<aol\>-\<type\>.nar, a compressed jar file containing machine
    specific parts of the distribution, such as the libraries. This file is specific to a particular
    Architecture-OS-Linker (AOL) combination <<TBD and is not generated if maven.nar.includefilesonly
    is set>>. Type specifies if this nar is either of type shared, static or jni.
    
    []

* {nar-integration-test}
               
    This goal runs tests against the packaged jar and nar files. Currently this is useful only to
test a jni library. The jni library is added to the java.library.path and the tests are forked to
pick up this path.

	To use this goal you need to put the test sources in the regular test directories but disable 
the running of the tests by the maven-surefire-plugin.
               
	<<TBD integration tests for other types of libraries than jni>>
                
* {install}                

	This goal installs the produced artifacts in your local repository. The unpacking is done in the 
unpack goal of a dependent artifact upon first usage.


* {deploy}
                
	This goal deploys the produced artifacts on a maven (web) server. The jar and nar files will always
be in their packaged form on the server (they will never be unpacked there by maven).

